WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.367 --> 00:00:01.401 line:92%
-Our first guest tonight i

00:00:01.401 --> 00:00:02.502 line:92%
an Emmy Award-winning acto

00:00:02.502 --> 00:00:03.236 size:81% position:41% line:92%
you know from his work

00:00:03.236 --> 00:00:04.571 align:start size:94% position:6%
in "The Birdcage,"
"Ray Donovan,"

00:00:04.571 --> 00:00:05.238 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
and "The Simpsons."

00:00:05.238 --> 00:00:07.407 align:start size:94% position:6%
All four seasons
of his show "Brockmire"

00:00:07.407 --> 00:00:08.408 line:92%
are streaming on Hulu now.

00:00:08.408 --> 00:00:11.044 align:start size:94% position:6%
Here's my conversation
with Hank Azaria.

00:00:11.044 --> 00:00:13.513 align:start size:94% position:6%
Welcome back, Hank.
How are ya?

00:00:13.513 --> 00:00:15.081 line:92%
-I'm hangin' in there, Seth.

00:00:15.081 --> 00:00:15.949 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
How you doin'?

00:00:15.949 --> 00:00:16.616 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-I'm doin' alright.

00:00:16.616 --> 00:00:20.553 align:start size:94% position:6%
I wanna start by talking
about the Mets.

00:00:20.553 --> 00:00:22.188 size:75% position:38% line:92%
Usually, we would be

00:00:22.188 --> 00:00:24.057 align:start size:94% position:6%
in a completely different
part of the season.

00:00:24.057 --> 00:00:25.558 align:start size:94% position:6%
You're both a Mets fan
and a Knicks fan

00:00:25.558 --> 00:00:30.397 align:start size:94% position:6%
and, with those teams,
has the pause on live sports

00:00:30.397 --> 00:00:31.631 align:start size:94% position:6%
been good
for your mental health,

00:00:31.631 --> 00:00:34.667 align:start size:94% position:6%
or do you still
desperately miss it?

00:00:34.667 --> 00:00:36.036 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Both.
I mean --

00:00:36.036 --> 00:00:38.338 size:81% position:41%
I saw somebody posted
online yesterday like,

00:00:38.338 --> 00:00:41.374 size:84% position:42%
"Wow! I watched a movie
tonight that I enjoyed,

00:00:41.374 --> 00:00:43.843 align:start size:94% position:6%
where, normally,
I would just have freaked ou

00:00:43.843 --> 00:00:46.112 align:start size:94% position:6%
and got wildly frustrated
with the Mets.

00:00:46.112 --> 00:00:49.616
I mean, what have I been
thinking all these years?

00:00:49.616 --> 00:00:52.118 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]
Peace in my life."

00:00:52.118 --> 00:00:54.854 align:start size:94% position:6%
Yeah, but, you know,
on the one hand, it's fun --

00:00:54.854 --> 00:00:59.092
it's a relief not to watch the
Knicks and Mets fail miserably

00:00:59.092 --> 00:01:00.760 align:start size:94% position:6%
On the other hand,
I'd sort of give anything

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:02.929 align:start size:94% position:6%
to watch them fail miserably
[ Laughing ] right now.

00:01:02.929 --> 00:01:05.632 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
My father-in-law loves music

00:01:05.632 --> 00:01:06.900 size:78% position:39%
the way I love sports
and I realized that,

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:09.469 align:start size:94% position:6%
when he goes to a concert,
there's never a chance

00:01:09.469 --> 00:01:10.370 line:92%
that he's gonna leave upset.

00:01:10.370 --> 00:01:12.372 align:start size:94% position:6%
Like the band is gonna
play the hits,

00:01:12.372 --> 00:01:15.442 align:start size:94% position:6%
whereas, just you're
flippin' a coin

00:01:15.442 --> 00:01:17.677 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughing ] when you go
see a sports game.

00:01:17.677 --> 00:01:19.245 line:92%
I wanna talk about "Brockmire"

00:01:19.245 --> 00:01:22.015 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I wanna get to like
how the character has evolved,

00:01:22.015 --> 00:01:26.586
but this started as sort o
a classic sketch character

00:01:26.586 --> 00:01:29.089 align:start size:94% position:6%
that has now --
which is incredible --

00:01:29.089 --> 00:01:30.390 align:start size:94% position:6%
endured four seasons
on television.

00:01:30.390 --> 00:01:33.393 align:start size:94% position:6%
But how did you first come u
with the character?

00:01:33.393 --> 00:01:35.595 align:start size:94% position:6%
-[ As Brockmire ]
This is the voice
of Jim Brockmire.

00:01:35.595 --> 00:01:40.266
I call it the generic '70s
baseball announcer voice.

00:01:40.266 --> 00:01:43.169 size:84% position:42%
And, you know, I was,
you know, 15 years old.

00:01:43.169 --> 00:01:46.172 align:start size:94% position:6%
I'm gonna tell you the story
as Jim Brockmire, Seth.

00:01:46.172 --> 00:01:49.742 align:start size:94% position:6%
I was 15 years old
and the year was 1979.

00:01:49.742 --> 00:01:52.245
And, right about then,
it started occurring to me that,

00:01:52.245 --> 00:01:55.315 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Why do all these guys
talk this way?"

00:01:55.315 --> 00:01:56.049 size:84% position:42% line:92%
Do they always sound --

00:01:56.049 --> 00:01:57.317 size:88% position:44%
Do they sound like this
in their personal lives?

00:01:57.317 --> 00:01:59.752 align:start size:94% position:6%
Like when they're dirty-talkin
their good lady wives

00:01:59.752 --> 00:02:03.256 align:start size:94% position:6%
during sex, does it still
come out this way?

00:02:03.256 --> 00:02:05.892 size:81% position:41%
Do they sound this way
when they're wasted?

00:02:05.892 --> 00:02:09.729 align:start size:94% position:6%
Also, Harry Shearer and I
at "The Simpsons" noticed --

00:02:09.729 --> 00:02:11.631 align:start size:94% position:6%
We talk a lot.
He does an amazing Vin Scully,

00:02:11.631 --> 00:02:13.466 align:start size:94% position:6%
Harry Shearer.
It's one of those impression

00:02:13.466 --> 00:02:15.168 align:start size:94% position:6%
that like you can't tell
the difference

00:02:15.168 --> 00:02:17.470 align:start size:94% position:6%
between actual Vin Scully
and Harry Shearer.

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:18.404 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-[ Laughing ] Sure.

00:02:18.404 --> 00:02:21.307 align:start size:94% position:6%
-And we noted
that baseball announcers

00:02:21.307 --> 00:02:23.176 align:start size:94% position:6%
can say pretty much
whatever they like,

00:02:23.176 --> 00:02:25.979 align:start size:94% position:6%
as long as they give the count
afterwards, you know, so.

00:02:25.979 --> 00:02:28.581 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ As Brockmire ]
Hey, I spent most
of last evening

00:02:28.581 --> 00:02:30.950 align:start size:94% position:6%
with an 8 ball of cocaine
and a prostitute.

00:02:30.950 --> 00:02:34.687
As Johnson swings and misses
at the breaking ball, 0-1.

00:02:34.687 --> 00:02:36.055 align:start size:94% position:6%
-[ Laughing ]
-And --

00:02:36.055 --> 00:02:37.757 size:75% position:38%
So, that became like
the comic premise,

00:02:37.757 --> 00:02:40.760 align:start size:94% position:6%
is just, "What weird stuff
can you say and give the count

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:44.097 size:84% position:42%
and then, do these guys
always talk like this?"

00:02:44.097 --> 00:02:45.031 line:92%
And you know what's funny?

00:02:45.031 --> 00:02:47.267 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know,
given your "SNL" roots,

00:02:47.267 --> 00:02:52.038 align:start size:94% position:6%
by the time I was about --
about 15 years ago maybe, like

00:02:52.038 --> 00:02:54.274 align:start size:94% position:6%
so I must've been about 40
in my early 40s

00:02:54.274 --> 00:02:55.909 align:start size:94% position:6%
and like I always wanted
to be on "SNL."

00:02:55.909 --> 00:02:59.812
I always felt like my skill se
would've been good over there.

00:02:59.812 --> 00:03:02.649 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Very much so. It would've
been a very good fit.

00:03:02.649 --> 00:03:04.484 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Never did I have
the opportunity.

00:03:04.484 --> 00:03:06.553 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I don't write
for myself very well,

00:03:06.553 --> 00:03:08.955 align:start size:94% position:6%
so, it just wasn't
a natural for me.

00:03:08.955 --> 00:03:11.457 align:start size:94% position:6%
And so, I walked
into my agent's office.

00:03:11.457 --> 00:03:12.792 align:start size:94% position:6%
I'm like, "You know,
I'm not on 'SNL.'

00:03:12.792 --> 00:03:15.228 align:start size:94% position:6%
Where can I have
these bunch of characters?

00:03:15.228 --> 00:03:15.929 size:75% position:38% line:92%
Where can I do 'em?"

00:03:15.929 --> 00:03:17.964 align:start size:94% position:6%
They were, like, "Well,
you know, will Ferrell created

00:03:17.964 --> 00:03:20.934
'Funny or Die' for almost
exactly this purpose."

00:03:20.934 --> 00:03:22.135 line:92%
-And it really does speak

00:03:22.135 --> 00:03:23.403 size:84% position:42%
to how funny the sketch
was in the beginning

00:03:23.403 --> 00:03:26.039 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I think, you know,
if you watch the pilot episode

00:03:26.039 --> 00:03:29.209 line:92%
it feels like sort of a sex romp

00:03:29.209 --> 00:03:32.278 align:start size:94% position:6%
and this funny idea
of a guy coming undone.

00:03:32.278 --> 00:03:37.951
And, yet, it's -- you know, yo
guys have attacked addiction

00:03:37.951 --> 00:03:39.919 line:92%
You've attacked relationships.

00:03:39.919 --> 00:03:42.121 align:start size:94% position:6%
And, I mean,
it's mind-blowing to me

00:03:42.121 --> 00:03:44.057 align:start size:94% position:6%
that you did this
fourth season --

00:03:44.057 --> 00:03:45.959 align:start size:94% position:6%
obviously, you filmed it
before any

00:03:45.959 --> 00:03:47.694 line:92%
of 2020 started getting crazy.

00:03:47.694 --> 00:03:51.631 align:start size:94% position:6%
But you jumped basically 10,
15 years into the future.

00:03:51.631 --> 00:03:54.767 align:start size:94% position:6%
Baseball is hanging on
for dear life.

00:03:54.767 --> 00:03:56.803 line:92%
The world is coming undone

00:03:56.803 --> 00:03:58.004 size:88% position:44% line:92%
I mean, do you guys talk

00:03:58.004 --> 00:03:59.572 align:start size:94% position:6%
about how far ahead
of the curve you were

00:03:59.572 --> 00:04:03.309
and how unfortunate, that yo
[ Laughing ] were so right

00:04:03.309 --> 00:04:08.281 size:84% position:42%
-I think it was wildly
precognitive, Season 4.

00:04:08.281 --> 00:04:10.583
Yeah, I mean, the first
five minutes of Season 4,

00:04:10.583 --> 00:04:13.419 align:start size:94% position:6%
you get this like
faux "Today" show opening

00:04:13.419 --> 00:04:16.155 line:92%
where there's a pandemic.

00:04:16.155 --> 00:04:17.957 line:92%
There's rioting in the streets

00:04:17.957 --> 00:04:20.026 align:start size:94% position:6%
What wasn't so hard to predict
ten years from now

00:04:20.026 --> 00:04:24.430 align:start size:94% position:6%
is that the average
temperature might be 170°°

00:04:24.430 --> 00:04:28.001 align:start size:94% position:6%
but the fact that baseball's
very near death.

00:04:28.001 --> 00:04:30.169
Who would've thought that
there'd be more baseball

00:04:30.169 --> 00:04:31.671 align:start size:94% position:6%
in a fictitious
ten years from now

00:04:31.671 --> 00:04:36.476 align:start size:94% position:6%
than there is
in the reality of 2020?

00:04:36.476 --> 00:04:37.377 line:92%
That's Joel Church-Cooper.

00:04:37.377 --> 00:04:41.281 size:88% position:44%
You know, my take on the
character was that romp,

00:04:41.281 --> 00:04:43.650 align:start size:94% position:6%
that raunchy romp,
that the short was --

00:04:43.650 --> 00:04:45.852 align:start size:94% position:6%
It was pretty sophomoric,
you know.

00:04:45.852 --> 00:04:48.988 align:start size:94% position:6%
I made the character
an alcoholic just

00:04:48.988 --> 00:04:52.458 align:start size:94% position:6%
to justify saying outlandish
[bleep]. Pardon me.

00:04:52.458 --> 00:04:55.962 align:start size:94% position:6%
-[ Laughing ]
-Outlandish crap on the air.

00:04:55.962 --> 00:05:00.667
But he saw the depth of a ma
struggling with his alcoholism

00:05:00.667 --> 00:05:02.802 align:start size:94% position:6%
and all that that implies
and trying to find --

00:05:02.802 --> 00:05:07.707
Like baseball, trying to fin
relevance in modern times.

00:05:07.707 --> 00:05:09.809 align:start size:94% position:6%
I'm, like, "What did you
write here, man?

00:05:09.809 --> 00:05:11.844 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's brilliant."
Yeah, I got lucky.

00:05:11.844 --> 00:05:14.547 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Well, I wonder like
what has it been like for you,

00:05:14.547 --> 00:05:16.683 align:start size:94% position:6%
as an actor, that you create
a character that,

00:05:16.683 --> 00:05:19.218 align:start size:94% position:6%
you know, not to, you know
devalue it at all,

00:05:19.218 --> 00:05:23.256
seems like a sketch character,
and then, you find this writer

00:05:23.256 --> 00:05:24.590 align:start size:94% position:6%
that's given him
all this depth

00:05:24.590 --> 00:05:27.627 align:start size:94% position:6%
and you're into it
as this really fun,

00:05:27.627 --> 00:05:28.861 size:81% position:41% line:92%
like vocal impression.

00:05:28.861 --> 00:05:32.198 align:start size:94% position:6%
Has that made it easier
for you to --

00:05:32.198 --> 00:05:32.932 size:84% position:42% line:92%
Because you're so great

00:05:32.932 --> 00:05:36.035 align:start size:94% position:6%
at playing the reality
of this person.

00:05:36.035 --> 00:05:37.170 line:92%
Starting with the silly voice,

00:05:37.170 --> 00:05:39.505 align:start size:94% position:6%
has it made playing
the dark parts easier,

00:05:39.505 --> 00:05:41.007 size:81% position:41% line:92%
or has it been harder?

00:05:41.007 --> 00:05:44.110 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Kind of both.
I mean, a lot of my gig

00:05:44.110 --> 00:05:45.178 line:92%
or my niche or my schtick,

00:05:45.178 --> 00:05:47.547 align:start size:94% position:6%
if you will,
is creating a silly voice

00:05:47.547 --> 00:05:50.216 align:start size:94% position:6%
and then seeing
if I can fill it in

00:05:50.216 --> 00:05:51.651 size:75% position:38% line:92%
as much as possible.

00:05:51.651 --> 00:05:52.385 size:88% position:44% line:92%
It's almost like a game.

00:05:52.385 --> 00:05:55.355 size:75% position:38%
Like can I make this
a real human being?

00:05:55.355 --> 00:05:56.489 line:92%
And it became a fun challenge.

00:05:56.489 --> 00:06:02.995 align:start size:94% position:6%
Like Amanda Peet,
who plays Brockmire's love

00:06:02.995 --> 00:06:06.432 align:start size:94% position:6%
she's great, and she's ver
real and raw, you know,

00:06:06.432 --> 00:06:08.735 align:start size:94% position:6%
and it's a great character
she's playing.

00:06:08.735 --> 00:06:11.237
So we'll have these intens
conversations in the show,

00:06:11.237 --> 00:06:13.573 align:start size:94% position:6%
where she's crying
and screaming and upset

00:06:13.573 --> 00:06:15.074 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I've gotta
answer her in kind,

00:06:15.074 --> 00:06:17.510
[ As Brockmire ]
but I've gotta say it like this.

00:06:17.510 --> 00:06:19.245 align:start size:94% position:6%
-[ Laughing ]
-I've gotta talk to her

00:06:19.245 --> 00:06:20.012 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
as from baseball.

00:06:20.012 --> 00:06:21.414 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I know, in my mind,
I'm like,

00:06:21.414 --> 00:06:23.249 line:92%
"Oh, Amanda's really upset

00:06:23.249 --> 00:06:23.950 line:92%
I gotta take care of her,

00:06:23.950 --> 00:06:26.386 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ As Brockmire ]
but it's gonna come
out of this filter.

00:06:26.386 --> 00:06:28.054 align:start size:94% position:6%
And can I make
that sound sincere?

00:06:28.054 --> 00:06:31.090
And, you know, that's been
the fun of the character.

00:06:31.090 --> 00:06:32.625 line:92%
-Well, you know what I do?

00:06:32.625 --> 00:06:33.993 align:start size:94% position:6%
I wanna tip my cap
to both of you

00:06:33.993 --> 00:06:34.961 align:start size:94% position:6%
because you stay
in the character

00:06:34.961 --> 00:06:37.730 align:start size:94% position:6%
and she has done
such a fantastic job of --

00:06:37.730 --> 00:06:38.398 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
she now accepts --

00:06:38.398 --> 00:06:39.899 align:start size:94% position:6%
you know, her character
accepts your character as real

00:06:39.899 --> 00:06:42.101
and that's what makes it
such a fun show to watch,

00:06:42.101 --> 00:06:45.238 align:start size:94% position:6%
as opposed to something
that I think could've been

00:06:45.238 --> 00:06:47.507 align:start size:94% position:6%
so much broader
and less effective.

00:06:47.507 --> 00:06:51.310 align:start size:94% position:6%
We'll be right back with mor
Hank, right after this.

